Work that is understood well enough to write down but has not been done.
Entries are dated and say what was established, so that picking one up does not
mean starting the investigation over.

Not a backlog of ideas -- something belongs here once the shape of the problem
is known and the open questions are named.


================================================================================
Read p'(psi) and FF'(psi) from NetCDF, including MaNTA output
Raised 2026-08-24.
================================================================================

  Today `meq::SplineProfile` reads one format: a text table of
  `psi  f(psi)  f'(psi)` triples, via `fromFile()` / `fromStream()`
  (src/meq/Profiles.cpp). Configuration reaches it through `[source]` with
  `SourceType = "mhd"` and the `PPrimeFile` / `GGPrimeFile` keys.

  Wanted: the same two profiles read from a NetCDF file, which may or may not
  have been produced by MaNTA (../MaNTA). So there are really two readers, or
  one reader with a configurable name mapping.

  WHAT THE MaNTA FORMAT ACTUALLY IS

  Established by reading ../MaNTA/NetCDFIO.{hpp,cpp} and dumping
  ../MaNTA/python/Tests/JAXAuxTest.ref.nc. It uses the netCDF C++4 API
  (`#include <netcdf>`, netCDF::NcFile). Layout:

      dimensions:  t = UNLIMITED          time
                   x = <n>                space
      variables:   double t(t)            "Time since start of simulation", s
                   double x(x)            the spatial coordinate
                   double nVariables      count, at root
      group Grid:  CellBoundaries(Index), PolyOrder
      group VarN:  u(t,x)     "Value"
                   q(t,x)     "Derivative"
                   sigma(t,x) "Flux"

  So each variable is a group named `Var0`, `Var1`, ... and each carries BOTH
  the value and its derivative on the `x` grid. That is promising, because a
  Hermite cubic wants exactly a value and a derivative per knot -- `meq::Knot`
  is `{psi, value, derivative}`, which is the same triple.

  THE FOUR QUESTIONS TO SETTLE BEFORE WRITING ANY OF IT

  1. It is a derivative SHIFT, not a match. MaNTA gives (f, df/dx). meq's
     `MHDSource` wants p' and gg' as profiles OF psi, and calls `prime()` on
     them for the Newton Jacobian -- so it needs (p', p'') and (gg', gg'').
     If MaNTA's stored variable is p, then its (u, q) gives (p, dp/dx), which is
     one derivative short of what meq needs. Options, in rough order of
     preference:
       (a) have the MaNTA physics case output p' directly as its own variable,
           so u = p' and q = p'' and the triple lines up exactly;
       (b) read (p, dp/dx) and differentiate the spline once more -- loses an
           order and makes the Jacobian noisier than the residual, which is
           precisely the failure mode that shows up as Newton converging
           linearly rather than quadratically;
       (c) read only values and fit a spline, discarding MaNTA's derivatives.
     (a) is much the best if the MaNTA side can be arranged. Decide this first;
     it determines everything else.

  2. What is `x`, and how does it map to psi in [0,1]? meq's profiles are
     normalised to psi in [0,1] and `SplineProfile` clamps outside that. MaNTA's
     `x` is a radial coordinate whose meaning is set by the physics case, and
     nothing in the file says which. Needs an explicit config key naming the
     convention, plus the normalisation, rather than a guess. If x is not psi
     the chain rule lands on top of question 1.

  3. Which group is which? The groups are `Var0`, `Var1`, ... -- generic. There
     is no metadata saying "this one is pressure". So the config must name the
     group or index for each of p' and gg'. Do not infer it from the
     `description` attribute, which is free text ("Variable 0").

  4. Which time slice? MaNTA is time dependent and meq wants one equilibrium.
     Default to the last slice, `t` index size-1, but make it configurable and
     record the value of `t` that was used in meq's own output -- an equilibrium
     computed from an unrecorded time slice is not reproducible.

  THE NON-MaNTA CASE

  "May or may not have come from MaNTA" means there must be a flat convention
  too: 1-D variables over a single dimension, something like `psi`, `pprime`,
  `ffprime`, with derivatives optional. Detect the MaNTA layout by structure --
  the `Grid` group, or `nVariables` at root -- and fall back to the flat one.
  Prefer sniffing over a config flag, but make the choice overridable, and say
  in the log which was used.

  MECHANICS

  - `libnetcdf_c++4` is installed (/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libnetcdf_c++4.so)
    along with the C library. MaNTA links it, so the CMake pattern is there.
  - MFEM's own MFEM_USE_NETCDF is NO in ../mfem-hdg-dev, which does not matter:
    meq would link netcdf directly. The pre-modernisation Makefile did exactly
    that (`-lnetcdf_c++4 -lnetcdf`).
  - The old tree had a NetCDFIO.hpp, deleted in the restructure. It is not worth
    recovering -- it was a class declaration missing its terminating semicolon,
    so it had never been compiled. `git show v0-legacy:NetCDFIO.hpp` if curious.
  - Keep the reader out of `Profiles.{hpp,cpp}`. Those two units are
    deliberately free of every dependency including MFEM, which is what lets
    them be unit tested on their own. A `ProfilesNetCDF.{hpp,cpp}` returning a
    `SplineProfile` keeps that property.

  TESTS

  Write a small NetCDF file in the test itself rather than committing a fixture,
  so the expected values are visible next to the assertions. Cover: the MaNTA
  layout, the flat layout, a missing group, a missing variable, a time index out
  of range, and non-monotonic `x`. And check the round trip against the text
  reader -- the same profile through both paths must agree.
